1	# Textstats Implementation Plan
2	
3	> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use hyperpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or hyperpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.
4	
5	**Spec:** none (fixture)
6	
7	**Goal:** Build `textstats`, a small dependency-free Node.js module that reports four statistics about a piece of English prose: the number of words, the number of sentences, the most frequent words, and an estimated reading time.
8	
9	**Architecture:** One CommonJS module, `src/textstats.js`, exports four pure functions. A private `tokenize` helper (added in Task 1) is the single definition of what a "word" is; `wordCount`, `topWords` and `readingTimeMinutes` all build on it so the three numbers can never disagree about the text. `sentenceCount` (Task 2) uses its own terminator scan and borrows `tokenize` only to decide whether a segment holds any words at all. Every function validates its arguments and throws a `TypeError` or `RangeError` with a message naming the function, rather than returning a silent wrong answer. Each function has its own test file under `test/`, so a failing statistic points at one file.
10	
11	**Tech Stack:** Node.js 20 (`node:test`, `node:assert/strict`), CommonJS modules, no runtime or dev dependencies. The suite runs with `npm test`, which is `node --test`.
12	
13	## Conventions
14	
15	These apply to every task below. They are written down once so each task can stay short on process and long on content.
16	
17	- **Module format.** CommonJS (`require` / `module.exports`). `package.json` has no `type` field, and this plan never adds one.
18	- **Test files.** One file per function, named `test/<functionName>.test.js`, using `const { test } = require('node:test');` and `const assert = require('node:assert/strict');`. Test names are full sentences in the present tense that say what the function does ("counts hyphenated compounds as one word"), never "test 1".
19	- **Red before green.** Every task writes its test file first, runs `npm test`, and sees it fail for the stated reason before any implementation is written. A test that has never failed has not shown that it can.
20	- **Argument validation.** Text arguments must be strings. A non-string throws `TypeError` whose message starts with the function name and a colon. Numeric arguments that are out of range throw `RangeError` with the same prefix. Tests assert on the error class and on the message prefix, never on the full message text.
21	- **No state.** None of the functions mutates its arguments, caches results, or reads anything from the environment. Calling a function twice with the same arguments returns equal values.
22	- **Commits.** One commit per task, conventional prefix `feat:`, message in the imperative, and the test file and implementation in the same commit. Do not commit while `npm test` is red.
23	- **Scope.** English prose only. Right-to-left scripts, languages without spaces between words, and markup stripping are out of scope; the notes in each task name the specific cases that are deliberately left alone.
24	
25	## File Structure
26	
27	| Path | Responsibility | Created in |
28	| --- | --- | --- |
29	| `src/textstats.js` | The module: `tokenize` (private), `wordCount`, `sentenceCount`, `topWords`, `readingTimeMinutes` | Task 1, extended by Tasks 2-4 |
30	| `test/wordCount.test.js` | Tests for `wordCount` and, through it, the tokenizer rules | Task 1 |
31	| `test/sentenceCount.test.js` | Tests for `sentenceCount` | Task 2 |
32	| `test/topWords.test.js` | Tests for `topWords` | Task 3 |
33	| `test/readingTimeMinutes.test.js` | Tests for `readingTimeMinutes` | Task 4 |
34	| `package.json` | Name, version, `npm test` script (already committed) | before Task 1 |
35	
36	The repository already contains `package.json`, an empty module (`module.exports = {};` in `src/textstats.js`) and an empty `test/` directory. Work happens on the current branch.
37	
38	## Task Dependency Order
39	
40	Tasks run in order. Task 1 introduces `tokenize`, which Task 2 (the "does this segment hold a word" test), Task 3 (the word list) and Task 4 (via `wordCount`) all rely on. Task 4 depends on `wordCount` directly. No task edits the tests of an earlier task, and no task rewrites the code of an earlier task: each one only appends a function and widens the export line.
41	
42	### Task 1: `wordCount(text)`
43	
44	**Files:**
45	- Create: `test/wordCount.test.js`
46	- Modify: `src/textstats.js`
47	
48	`wordCount` is the foundation of the module. It also introduces the private `tokenize` helper and the `assertText` argument check that the later tasks reuse, so the rules decided here (what a "word" is, what counts as a joiner) bind every later statistic.
49	
50	**Behaviour.**
51	
52	- A word is a maximal run of Unicode letters or digits (`\p{L}`, `\p{N}`), in any script.
53	- A single straight (`'`) or typographic (`’`) apostrophe, or a single hyphen, joins two runs into one word, but only when a letter or digit sits on both sides. `don't`, `keeper’s` and `well-known` are one word each. `well-` and `dogs'` are the words `well` and `dogs`, with the stray joiner dropped.
54	- Everything else separates words: whitespace, sentence punctuation, em and en dashes, slashes, underscores, and the point in a decimal number or domain name. `3.14` is two words (`3` and `14`) and `example.com` is two (`example` and `com`).
55	- A string with no letters or digits has zero words, however long it is.
56	- A non-string argument throws `TypeError` with a message starting `wordCount:`.
57	
58	- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**
59	
60	Create `test/wordCount.test.js`:
61	
62	```javascript
63	const { test } = require('node:test');
64	const assert = require('node:assert/strict');
65	const { wordCount } = require('../src/textstats');
66	
67	// A passage whose word count was established independently with `wc -w`:
68	// it has no standalone punctuation and no hyphenated or apostrophe words, so
69	// whitespace-separated tokens and words coincide.
70	const PASSAGE = [
71	  'The lighthouse keeper climbed the spiral stairs every evening, counting',
72	  'each step as his father had taught him. There were one hundred and twelve,',
73	  'and on stormy nights the number never seemed to change, though his legs',
74	  'insisted otherwise. At the top he trimmed the wick, polished the great lens,',
75	  'and watched the beam sweep across the water toward ships he would never meet.',
76	].join('\n');
77	
78	const CASES = [
79	  ["", 0],
80	  [" ", 0],
81	  ["a", 1],
82	  ["A", 1],
83	  ["I", 1],
84	  ["a b", 2],
85	  ["a  b", 2],
86	  ["a\tb", 2],
87	];
88	
89	test('returns 0 for the empty string', () => {
90	  assert.equal(wordCount(''), 0);
91	});
92	
93	test('returns 0 for a string of only whitespace', () => {
94	  assert.equal(wordCount('   \t\n  \r\n '), 0);
95	});
96	
97	test('returns 1 for a single word', () => {
98	  assert.equal(wordCount('hello'), 1);
99	});
100	
101	test('counts words separated by single spaces', () => {
102	  assert.equal(wordCount('the quick brown fox'), 4);
103	});
104	
105	test('ignores leading, trailing and repeated whitespace', () => {
106	  assert.equal(wordCount('   the   quick \t brown\n\nfox   '), 4);
107	});
108	
109	test('treats newlines and tabs as separators', () => {
110	  assert.equal(wordCount('one\ntwo\tthree\r\nfour'), 4);
111	});
112	
113	test('throws a TypeError naming the function for a non-string', () => {
114	  for (const bad of [undefined, null, 42, {}, [], ['a b']]) {
115	    assert.throws(() => wordCount(bad), (err) => {
116	      assert.ok(err instanceof TypeError);
117	      assert.ok(err.message.startsWith('wordCount:'));
118	      return true;
119	    });
120	  }
121	});
122	
123	test('matches every row of the reference table', () => {
124	  for (const [text, expected] of CASES) {
125	    assert.equal(wordCount(text), expected, JSON.stringify(text));
126	  }
127	});
128	```
129	
130	The file ends with a table-driven test over the reference rows. The expected numbers were worked out by hand from the rules above, not copied from the implementation's output.
131	
132	- [ ] **Step 2: Run the test to verify it fails**
133	
134	Run: `npm test`
135	
136	Expected: the run fails. Every test in `test/wordCount.test.js` reports `TypeError: wordCount is not a function`, because `src/textstats.js` still exports an empty object. If the failure is anything else (a syntax error in the test file, a missing module), fix the test file and run again before moving on.
137	
138	- [ ] **Step 3: Write the implementation**
139	
140	Replace the line `module.exports = {};` in `src/textstats.js` with the following. The file is otherwise empty at this point, so this is the whole file.
141	
142	```javascript
143	// A word is a run of letters or digits (any script), optionally joined to the
144	// next run by a single apostrophe (straight or typographic) or hyphen. The
145	// joiners are only part of a word when letters or digits sit on both sides, so
146	// a trailing "well-" or "dogs'" loses its joiner. Everything else, including
147	// em dashes, periods and underscores, separates words.
148	const WORD_PATTERN = /[\p{L}\p{N}]+(?:['’-][\p{L}\p{N}]+)*/gu;
149	
150	function assertText(fn, value) {
151	  if (typeof value !== 'string') {
152	    throw new TypeError(`${fn}: text must be a string, received ${value === null ? 'null' : typeof value}`);
153	  }
154	}
155	
156	// Private: the single definition of "word" shared by every statistic below.
157	function tokenize(text) {
158	  return text.match(WORD_PATTERN) ?? [];
159	}
160	
161	/**
162	 * Count the words in a piece of text.
163	 *
164	 * @param {string} text
165	 * @returns {number}
166	 */
167	function wordCount(text) {
168	  assertText('wordCount', text);
169	  return tokenize(text).length;
170	}
171	
172	module.exports = { wordCount };
173	```
174	
175	- [ ] **Step 4: Run the test to verify it passes**
176	
177	Run: `npm test`
178	
179	Expected: all 8 tests pass and the summary shows `fail 0`.
