188	### Task 2: `sentenceCount(text)`
189	
190	**Files:**
191	- Create: `test/sentenceCount.test.js`
192	- Modify: `src/textstats.js`
193	
194	`sentenceCount` splits prose at sentence terminators while avoiding the three most common false breaks: decimal numbers, dotted names such as `example.com`, and abbreviations like `Mr.` and `e.g.`. It reuses `tokenize` and `assertText` from Task 1 and appends to `src/textstats.js` without changing anything already there.
195	
196	**Behaviour.**
197	
198	- A terminator is a run of `.`, `!` or `?` (so `?!` and `...` are one terminator), optionally followed by closing quotes or brackets, and only when whitespace or the end of the text follows it. A period inside `3.14`, `example.com` or `a.b.c` has no whitespace after it and is not a terminator.
199	- A lone `.` that follows one of the listed abbreviations (`Mr`, `Mrs`, `Ms`, `Dr`, `Prof`, `St`, `Sr`, `Jr`, `vs`, `e.g`, `i.e`, compared without regard to case) does not end a sentence. Runs such as `...` or `?!` always do.
200	- A sentence is counted only if it contains at least one word, so a text made only of terminators has zero sentences.
201	- Text after the last terminator counts as a final sentence when it contains a word.
202	- A non-string argument throws `TypeError` with a message starting `sentenceCount:`.
203	
204	- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**
205	
206	Create `test/sentenceCount.test.js`:
207	
208	```javascript
209	const { test } = require('node:test');
210	const assert = require('node:assert/strict');
